@title Emotional Dysregulation Therapy
## helping-your-child-or-teenager-understand-and-manage-big-emo |  | Helping Your Child or Teenager Understand and Manage Big Emotions | Helping Your Child or Teenager Understand and Manage Big Emotions
@intro
Some children and teenagers experience emotions very intensely.
They may become overwhelmed quickly, have difficulty calming down after becoming upset, react strongly to situations that seem small to others, or find it difficult to put their feelings into words.
For parents, it can be difficult to know what is behind these reactions or how best to respond.
Emotional regulation is a skill that can be developed.
Therapy can help children and teenagers understand their emotional experiences, recognise what is happening before emotions become overwhelming, and develop strategies for responding in ways that better support their wellbeing and relationships.
## what-is-emotional-dysregulation |  | What Is Emotional Dysregulation? | What Is Emotional Dysregulation?
Emotion regulation refers to the ability to influence and manage our emotional state. This does not mean preventing emotions or never becoming upset.
Everyone experiences anger, frustration, sadness, anxiety and excitement.
The difficulty can arise when emotions become so intense, persistent or difficult to manage that they begin affecting everyday life, relationships, learning or participation in activities.
Emotional dysregulation can look different from one person to another.
A child or teenager may:
- Become overwhelmed quickly
- Have intense emotional reactions
- Move rapidly from one emotion to another
- Struggle to calm down after becoming upset
- React impulsively when emotions are strong
- Find it difficult to identify or describe what they are feeling
- Become frustrated when things do not go as expected
- Become stuck in repetitive negative thinking
- Withdraw when emotionally overwhelmed
- Find conflict particularly difficult
- Regret things they have said or done after becoming calmer
These experiences do not automatically mean a child has a mental health disorder.
Understanding what is driving the difficulty is an important part of assessment and treatment.
## when-big-feelings-become-hard-to-manage |  | When Big Feelings Become Hard to Manage | When Big Feelings Become Hard to Manage
A child might cope well most of the time but become overwhelmed when they are tired, stressed, disappointed, frustrated or faced with an unexpected change.
For another child, emotional regulation difficulties may occur more frequently and affect school, friendships or family life.
You may notice:
#### At home
- Frequent emotional outbursts
- Difficulty accepting limits or changes
- Arguments that escalate quickly
- Difficulty moving on after becoming upset
- Needing significant support to calm down
#### At school
- Difficulty managing frustration
- Emotional reactions to mistakes or perceived criticism
- Problems concentrating when emotionally overwhelmed
- Conflict with peers or teachers
- Difficulty transitioning between activities
#### With friendships
- Strong reactions to disagreements
- Difficulty interpreting social situations
- Impulsive responses during conflict
- Feeling rejected or misunderstood
- Difficulty repairing relationships after an emotional reaction
## what-can-contribute-to-emotional-dysregulation |  | What Can Contribute to Emotional Dysregulation? | What Can Contribute to Emotional Dysregulation?
Emotional regulation difficulties can have many possible contributors.
They may be associated with:
- Stress or significant life changes
- Anxiety or persistent worry
- Difficulties with attention or impulse control
- ADHD symptoms
- Neurodevelopmental differences
- Challenges with identifying or expressing emotions
- Sleep difficulties
- Difficulties in relationships or social situations
- Experiences that have been emotionally overwhelming
The relationship between emotional regulation and ADHD is particularly relevant for some young people. Current ACAMH guidance notes that adolescents with ADHD symptoms may experience intense or rapidly changing emotions, strong emotional reactions and emotionally driven behaviours.
However, emotional dysregulation is not the same thing as ADHD, and a child's presentation should be considered individually.
## emotional-dysregulation-and-adhd |  | Emotional Dysregulation and ADHD | Emotional Dysregulation and ADHD
For some adolescents with ADHD symptoms, difficulties with emotional regulation can add to the challenges they are already experiencing.
They may find it difficult to recognise emotions as they arise, shift away from emotionally charged thoughts, pause before reacting, or choose a helpful response when emotions are intense.
ACAMH's 2026 guidance highlights several skills that may support emotional regulation, including acceptance, cognitive reappraisal, grounding, reducing rumination and learning to tolerate strong emotions without immediately acting on them.
Importantly, the goal is not to make a young person stop having strong emotions.
It is to help them develop a larger repertoire of ways to respond.
## you-may-be-looking-for-support-because |  | You May Be Looking for Support Because... | You May Be Looking for Support Because...
You may be considering therapy because your child or teenager:
- Seems to have emotions that feel "too big" for the situation
- Becomes overwhelmed easily
- Has frequent conflicts at home or school
- Finds frustration particularly difficult
- Struggles to calm down once upset
- Acts impulsively when emotional
- Has difficulty explaining what they are feeling
- Is experiencing problems with friendships
- Becomes stuck in negative thought patterns
- Is finding everyday situations increasingly difficult to manage
- Has ADHD or ADHD symptoms alongside emotional regulation difficulties
You may also simply have a sense that your child is struggling to manage emotions in ways that are affecting their everyday life.
You don't need to know the exact reason before seeking support.
## how-can-therapy-help |  | How Can Therapy Help? | How Can Therapy Help?
Therapy can give a child or teenager a safe space to understand their emotional experiences without being judged for having strong feelings.
Depending on their age and individual needs, therapy may focus on:
### Recognising emotions
Learning to notice emotional and physical signs that indicate an emotion is building.
### Naming and communicating feelings
Developing language to explain what they are experiencing and what they need.
### Understanding emotional triggers
Identifying situations, thoughts, relationships or patterns that tend to increase emotional intensity.
### Cognitive reappraisal
Learning to consider alternative or more balanced ways of interpreting emotionally difficult situations. This is commonly incorporated into CBT-based approaches.
### Acceptance and mindfulness
Learning that an emotion can be acknowledged without immediately needing to act on it.
### Grounding and attention strategies
Developing practical ways to reconnect with the present moment when emotions become intense.
### Problem-solving and communication
Building skills for responding to difficult situations, managing disagreements and asking for support.
### Building emotional flexibility
Learning that different situations may require different regulation strategies rather than relying on one response every time.
## what-about-dbt |  | What About DBT? | What About DBT?
Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) includes skills relevant to emotional regulation, distress tolerance, mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness.
For some adolescents, DBT-informed strategies may form part of psychological therapy when clinically appropriate.
However, DBT is not automatically appropriate for every child or teenager experiencing emotional dysregulation. The type of therapy should be matched to the young person's individual presentation, developmental stage and treatment needs.
ACAMH's discussion of DBT for adolescents highlights its relevance to emotion regulation and related difficulties while also considering the developing evidence base and the importance of adapting approaches appropriately for young people.
## for-parents-you-can-help-your-child-learn-regulation |  | For Parents: You Can Help Your Child Learn Regulation | For Parents: You Can Help Your Child Learn Regulation
Children develop emotional regulation partly through their relationships with supportive adults.
Parents can help by:
- Staying as calm as possible when their child is overwhelmed
- Helping the child identify and name emotions
- Acknowledging the feeling without automatically agreeing with every reaction
- Modelling healthy ways of responding to frustration
- Talking about emotions when everyone is calm
- Helping the child recognise patterns and triggers
- Encouraging age-appropriate problem-solving
- Allowing opportunities to practise skills rather than expecting immediate perfection
Parents do not have to prevent every difficult emotion.
The goal is to help children gradually learn:
"I can feel this emotion, understand what is happening, and learn what to do next."
Psychology Today similarly highlights the role of parents in modelling coping strategies and helping children identify, label and appropriately express emotions.
## what-could-change-with-the-right-support |  | What Could Change With the Right Support? | What Could Change With the Right Support?
With appropriate support, a child or teenager may begin to:
- Understand their emotional responses more clearly
- Recognise emotions earlier
- Identify triggers and patterns
- Develop practical regulation strategies
- Pause before reacting
- Communicate feelings more effectively
- Handle frustration more flexibly
- Manage difficult social situations
- Reduce conflict at home or school
- Recover more effectively after becoming upset
- Build confidence in their ability to cope
The aim is not emotional perfection.
Everyone becomes angry, disappointed, worried or overwhelmed sometimes.
The goal is to help a young person develop the skills to navigate those experiences more effectively.
## when-should-you-seek-professional-support |  | When Should You Seek Professional Support? | When Should You Seek Professional Support?
Consider seeking professional support when emotional regulation difficulties are persistent or are beginning to interfere with your child's everyday life.
This may include difficulties affecting:
- Family relationships
- Friendships
- School participation
- Learning and concentration
- Emotional wellbeing
- Behaviour
- Confidence
- Daily routines
You do not have to wait until you have a diagnosis or know exactly what is causing the difficulties.
An appropriate practitioner can help explore what may be contributing to your child's presentation and whether psychological therapy may be helpful.
## you-dont-need-to-know-where-to-start |  | You Don't Need to Know Where to Start | You Don't Need to Know Where to Start
As a parent, it can be difficult to know whether your child's emotional reactions are part of normal development, related to stress, connected with ADHD or another concern, or simply something they need help learning to manage.
You don't need to diagnose the problem before reaching out.
At MBP, we can help you understand your options and identify an appropriate practitioner based on your child's or teenager's age, presenting concerns and available services.
## why-families-choose-mbp |  | Why Families Choose MBP | Why Families Choose MBP
When your child is struggling with emotions, finding the right type of support can feel overwhelming.
MBP can help families navigate the first steps by:
- Listening to your concerns
- Understanding your child's individual circumstances
- Helping identify appropriate practitioner options
- Considering age and presenting needs
